# Pin amdkde's whole toolchain to gcc-15 (block gcc-16 as the selected
# compiler). This layer is amdkde-ONLY — it is not in the 'nvidia' layer that
# amdkde and nvidiahard share, precisely so the two can diverge here.
#
# Why amdkde stays gcc-15 when nvidiahard goes gcc-16:
#   amdkde clones this host and REUSES the host's binpkg pool
#   (PROF_LIVE_BINPKGS_DIR=/var/cache/binpkgs, FEATURES=getbinpkg+buildpkg).
#   The host is gcc-15 (it never installed gcc-16). If amdkde built its desktop
#   with gcc-16, its buildpkg output would poison that shared pool with gcc-16
#   binaries the gcc-15 host cannot later consume — a package built with gcc-16
#   links libstdc++ symbol versions absent from the host's gcc-15 runtime, so
#   the host's own '--usepkg' would fail at merge with an undefined-symbol
#   error. Keeping amdkde on gcc-15 keeps the pool coherent in BOTH directions.
#
# The hardened images do NOT take this layer. amdkdehard has no CUDA and builds
# gcc-16 as the tree intends; nvidiahard builds gcc-16 for the desktop and
# forces gcc-15 only on the CUDA packages via package.env/gcc15.conf. They
# share a private pool (binpkgs-amdkdehard), both gcc-16 at the desktop, so
# that pool is coherent too. This split is why the blanket mask left the
# shared 'nvidia' layer.
#
# The cap is nvcc's, not nvidia-drivers': the driver deps on 'sys-devel/gcc:*'
# and builds its module with whatever gcc compiled the kernel, so it is fine on
# any slot. nvcc (nvidia-cuda-toolkit-13.3.1) is what refuses host gcc > 15.
#
# Drop this file when the host itself moves to gcc-16 (then amdkde can follow
# the tree like the hardened images, and the pool stays coherent at gcc-16).
>=sys-devel/gcc-16
